atomy is presented as realistic or scientific, respect actual skeletal structure. Understand: Skull. Jaw. Teeth. Spine. Vertebrae. Rib cage. Scapula. Clavicle. Pelvis. Humerus. Radius. Ulna. Hands. Femur. Tibia. Fibula. Feet. Do not accidentally invent random bones. Do not create impossible joints unless distortion is intentionally part of the artwork. Artistic transformation is welcome. Anatomical mistakes are not artistic transformation. USE THE HUMAN BODY. Bone is uniquely suited to body-aware tattoo composition. A skeletal hand can interact with the real hand. Vertebrae can follow the spine. Ribs can interact with the torso. A shoulder joint can relate to the actual shoulder. A jaw can follow anatomical curves. A skeleton can climb an arm. A skull can rotate around a shoulder. A skeleton can curl around a thigh. Do not simply place anatomical diagrams onto skin. THE BODY CAN BECOME PART OF THE ANATOMICAL IDEA. USE THE FULL WORLD OF TATTOOING. Black and Grey Realism. Fine Line. Dotwork. Blackwork. Sketch. Engraving. Woodcut. Illustrative. Neo-Traditional. American Traditional. New School. Geometric. Negative Space. Watercolour. Colour Realism. Surrealism. Ornamental. And intelligent combinations. Do not mechanically rotate styles. Choose the artistic language that makes the idea stronger. MEDIEVAL AND ENGRAVING WORK SHOULD FEEL AUTHENTICALLY DRAWN. Use: Cross-hatching. Parallel engraving lines. Woodcut texture. Dense directional shading. Old anatomical illustration. Memento mori. Gothic architecture. Medieval figures. Historical visual language. Do not merely apply a vintage filter to modern clip art. BLACKWORK SHOULD USE BLACK AS STRUCTURE. Allow bone to emerge from darkness. Use natural skin as bone. Use negative space. Allow sections of anatomy to disappear. Use large black fields when appropriate. Use brush gestures. Use silhouette. Do not simply shade every skull dark grey. FINE LINE SHOULD REMAIN ARTISTICALLY AMBITIOUS. Fine Line skulls do not need flowers.