$b['A reconstructed prehistoric skeleton runs through a museum-like darkness but progressively gains muscle and living form toward the head as though the fossil is becoming the animal again'] = 'Two panels side by side on one image, divided down the middle. LEFT PANEL: the tattoo design alone on a plain white background, shown flat exactly as described, no body, drawn large so it fills most of the panel. RIGHT PANEL: Studio photograph of a real person''s body showing the same exact tattoo where described, healed tattoo on real skin in soft even light, the rest of the body modestly covered by simple plain clothing, only the tattooed area bare, plain seamless light grey studio backdrop - no room, no furniture, no scenery. The tattoo in both panels is identical. The tattoo: A reconstructed prehistoric skeleton runs through a museum-like darkness, but progressively gains muscle and living form toward the head as though the fossil is becoming the animal again. Prehistoric, transformative, cinematic. In the right panel it is on a man, outer thigh. Create exceptional contemporary tattoo inspiration centered on skulls, skeletons and bone. BONE IS THE MATERIAL. DEATH IS NOT THE ONLY SUBJECT. Skulls and skeletons can express: Anatomy. Death. Life. Movement. Love. Loneliness. Strength. Fear. Beauty. Time. Nature. Science. History. Transformation. Darkness. Faith. Architecture. And pure fascination with skeletal form. UNDERSTAND THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A SKULL AND A SKELETON. A skull is primarily a form. Study it. Rotate it. Light it. Fracture it. Abstract it. Open it. Reconstruct it. Transform it. Use unusual anatomical viewpoints. Do not default to a centered front-facing skull. A skeleton is a body. USE BODY LANGUAGE. Skeletons can: Walk. Run. Dance. Fight. Pray. Reach. Fall. Climb. Embrace. Protect. Carry. Swim. Ride. Rest. Lie down. Curl inward. Stretch upward. Skeletons do not need facial expression to communicate emotion. Posture is the expression. ANATOMY MATTERS. When a