ack fields when appropriate. Use brush gestures. Use silhouette. Do not simply shade every skull dark grey. FINE LINE SHOULD REMAIN ARTISTICALLY AMBITIOUS. Fine Line skulls do not need flowers. Explore unusual anatomical angles. Skeletal hands. Spines. Pelvis. Movement. Full skeletons. Relationships between figures. Scientific drawing. Construction. COLOR IS WELCOME. Bone does not need to be painted random colors. Color can come from light. Neon. Water. Fire. Stained glass. Sunset. Reflections. Atmosphere. Environmental light. Ivory bone can receive: Cobalt light. Crimson light. Emerald light. Violet shadow. Turquoise underwater reflection. Warm gold. Color should make physical or artistic sense. Do not turn every colorful skull into a sugar skull. DIA DE LOS MUERTOS AND CALAVERA IMAGERY HAVE SPECIFIC CULTURAL CONTEXT. Treat those traditions accurately and respectfully when intentionally used. Do not use Mexican cultural imagery merely as generic colorful skull decoration. ANIMAL SKELETONS BELONG. Explore: Bird skeletons. Snake skeletons. Bat skeletons. Horse skeletons. Whale skeletons. Ram skulls. Deer skulls. Crocodile skulls. Shark jaws. Prehistoric skeletons. Other fascinating skeletal anatomy. Do not simply repeat the Animals category with bones. The skeletal structure itself should be the artistic reason for the design. SURREALISM CAN GO FAR. A skull can contain architecture. An eye socket can become a doorway. A rib cage can contain an ocean. A skeleton can become roots. A skull can contain a storm. Bone can become birds. Bone can become architecture. Bone can become geometry. A skeleton can escape another skeleton. A skull can separate into layers. Scale can become impossible. But maintain visual hierarchy. Do not create random surreal collage. THE SKULL OR SKELETON MUST REMAIN THE HERO. DARKNESS IS WELCOME. Do not make everything dark. Do not make everything beautiful. Do not make everything aggressive. Do not make everything solemn. Bone can be: Quiet. Romantic. Scientific. Sexual. Tender. Brutal. Elegant. Terrifying. Peaceful. Funny when intentionally appropriate. Ancient. Futuristic. Minimal. Monumental. THE CLICHES ARE ALLOWED. THE CLICHES ARE NOT ALLOWED TO CONTROL THE CATEGORY. Skull and rose. Skull and snake. Skull and crown. Skull and dagger. Skull and moth. Skull and clock. These are established tattoo subjects. Use them when the design is genuinely exceptional. Do not produce endless variations because they are easy. Do not surround every skull with flowers. Do not put a crown on every skull. Do not put a snake through every eye socket. Do not make every skeleton a grim reaper. Do not make every skeleton comedic. Do not make every skull realistic. Do not make every design Black and Grey. Do not make every skull face forward. Do not make every skeleton stand upright. Do not make everything about death. BONE IS THE MATERIAL. THE ART CAN GO ANYWHERE'
